#: ../../../users/faq.rst:6
msgid "FAQ"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:9
msgid "General"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:12
msgid "What is Syncthing?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:14
msgid ""
"Syncthing is an application that lets you synchronize your files across "
"multiple devices. This means the creation, modification or deletion of files "
"on one machine will automatically be replicated to your other devices. We "
"believe your data is your data alone and you deserve to choose where it is "
"stored. Therefore Syncthing does not upload your data to the cloud but "
"exchanges your data across your machines as soon as they are online at the "
"same time."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:22
msgid "Is it \"syncthing\", \"Syncthing\" or \"SyncThing\"?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:24
msgid ""
"It's **Syncthing**, although the command and source repository is spelled "
"``syncthing`` so it may be referred to in that way as well. It's definitely "
"not :strike:`SyncThing`, even though the abbreviation ``st`` is used in some "
"circumstances and file names."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:30
msgid "How does Syncthing differ from BitTorrent Sync?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:32
msgid ""
"The two are different and not related. Syncthing and BitTorrent Sync "
"accomplish some of the same things, namely syncing files between two or more "
"computers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:35
msgid ""
"BitTorrent Sync by BitTorrent, Inc is a proprietary peer-to-peer file "
"synchronization tool available for Windows, Mac, Linux, Android, iOS, "
"Windows Phone, Amazon Kindle Fire and BSD. [#btsync]_ Syncthing is an open "
"source file synchronization tool."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:40
msgid ""
"Syncthing uses an open and documented protocol, and likewise the security "
"mechanisms in use are well defined and visible in the source code. "
"BitTorrent Sync uses an undocumented, closed protocol with unknown security "
"properties."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:44
msgid "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BitTorrent_Sync"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:47
msgid "Usage"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:50
msgid "What things are synced?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:52
msgid "The following things are *always* synchronized:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:54
msgid "File Contents"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:55
msgid "File Modification Times"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:57
msgid "The following may be synchronized or not, depending:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:59
msgid ""
"File Permissions (When supported by file system. On Windows, only the read "
"only bit is synchronized.)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:61
msgid ""
"Symbolic Links (When supported by the OS. On Windows Vista and up, requires "
"administrator privileges. Links are synced as is and are not followed.)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:65
msgid "The following is *not* synchronized;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:67
msgid "File or Directory Owners and Groups (not preserved)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:68
msgid "Directory Modification Times (not preserved)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:69
msgid "Hard Links (followed, not preserved)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:70
msgid "Extended Attributes, Resource Forks (not preserved)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:71
msgid "Windows, POSIX or NFS ACLs (not preserved)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:72
msgid "Devices, FIFOs, and Other Specials (ignored)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:73
msgid "Sparse file sparseness (will become unsparse)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:76
msgid "Is synchronization fast?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:78
msgid ""
"Syncthing segments files into pieces, called blocks, to transfer data from "
"one device to another. Therefore, multiple devices can share the "
"synchronization load, in a similar way as the torrent protocol. The more "
"devices you have online (and synchronized), the faster an additional device "
"will receive the data because small blocks will be fetched from all devices "
"in parallel."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:84
msgid ""
"Syncthing handles renaming files and updating their metadata in an efficient "
"manner. This means that renaming a large file will not cause a "
"retransmission of that file. Additionally, appending data to existing large "
"files should be handled efficiently as well."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:89
msgid ""
"Temporary files are used to store partial data downloaded from other "
"devices. They are automatically removed whenever a file transfer has been "
"completed or after the configured amount of time which is set in the "
"configuration file (24 hours by default)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:95
msgid "Why is the sync so slow?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:97
msgid ""
"When troubleshooting a slow sync, there are a number of things to check."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:99
msgid ""
"First of all, verify that you are not connected via a relay. In the \"Remote "
"Devices\" list on the right side of the GUI, double check that you see "
"\"Address: <some address>\" and *not* \"Relay: <some address>\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:105
msgid ""
"If you are connected via a relay, this is because a direct connection could "
"not be established. Double check and follow the suggestions in :ref:"
"`firewall-setup` to enable direct connections."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:109
msgid ""
"Second, if one of the devices is a very low powered machine (a Raspberry Pi, "
"or a phone, or a NAS, or similar) you are likely constrained by the CPU on "
"that device. See the next question for reasons Syncthing likes a faster CPU. "
"You can verify this by looking at the CPU utilization in the GUI. If it is "
"constantly at or close to 100%, you are limited by the CPU speed. In some "
"cases a lower CPU usage number can also indicate being limited by the CPU - "
"for example constant 25% usage on a four core CPU likely means that "
"Syncthing is doing something that is not parallellizable and thus limited to "
"a single CPU core."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:119
msgid ""
"Third, verify that the network connection is OK. Tools such as iperf or just "
"an Internet speed test can be used to verify the performance here."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:123
msgid "Why does it use so much CPU?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:125
msgid ""
"When new or changed files are detected, or Syncthing starts for the first "
"time, your files are hashed using SHA-256."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:128
msgid ""
"Data that is sent over the network is (optionally) compressed and encrypted "
"using AES-128. When receiving data, it must be decrypted."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:131
msgid ""
"There is a certain amount of housekeeping that must be done to track the "
"current and available versions of each file in the index database."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:134
msgid ""
"By default Syncthing uses periodic scanning every 60 seconds to detect file "
"changes. This means checking every file's modification time and comparing it "
"to the database. This can cause spikes of CPU usage for large folders."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:139
msgid ""
"Hashing, compression and encryption cost CPU time. Also, using the GUI "
"causes a certain amount of extra CPU usage to calculate the summary data it "
"presents. Note however that once things are *in sync* CPU usage should be "
"negligible."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:144
msgid ""
"To limit the amount of CPU used when syncing and scanning, set the "
"environment variable ``GOMAXPROCS`` to the maximum number of CPU cores "
"Syncthing should use at any given moment. For example, ``GOMAXPROCS=2`` on a "
"machine with four cores will limit Syncthing to no more than half the "
"system's CPU power."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:150
msgid ""
"To reduce CPU spikes from scanning activity, use a filesystem notifications "
"plugin. This is delivered by default via Synctrayzor, Syncthing-GTK and on "
"Android. For other setups, consider using `syncthing-inotify <https://github."
"com/syncthing/syncthing-inotify>`_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:156
msgid "Should I keep my device IDs secret?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:158
msgid ""
"No. The IDs are not sensitive. Given a device ID it's possible to find the "
"IP address for that node, if global discovery is enabled on it. Knowing the "
"device ID doesn't help you actually establish a connection to that node or "
"get a list of files, etc."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:163
msgid ""
"For a connection to be established, both nodes need to know about the "
"other's device ID. It's not possible (in practice) to forge a device ID. (To "
"forge a device ID you need to create a TLS certificate with that specific "
"SHA-256 hash. If you can do that, you can spoof any TLS certificate. The "
"world is your oyster!)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:170
msgid ":ref:`device-ids`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:173
msgid "What if there is a conflict?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:175
msgid ""
"Syncthing does recognize conflicts. When a file has been modified on two "
"devices simultaneously, one of the files will be renamed to ``<filename>."
"sync- conflict-<date>-<time>.<ext>``. The device which has the larger value "
"of the first 63 bits for his device ID will have his file marked as the "
"conflicting file. Note that we only create ``sync-conflict`` files when the "
"actual content differs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:182
msgid ""
"Beware that the ``<filename>.sync-conflict-<date>-<time>.<ext>`` files are "
"treated as normal files after they are created, so they are propagated "
"between devices. We do this because the conflict is detected and resolved on "
"one device, creating the ``sync-conflict`` file, but it's just as much of a "
"conflict everywhere else and we don't know which of the conflicting files is "
"the \"best\" from the user point of view. Moreover, if there's something "
"that automatically causes a conflict on change you'll end up with ``sync-"
"conflict-...sync-conflict -...-sync-conflict`` files."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:192
msgid "How to configure multiple users on a single machine?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:194
msgid ""
"Each user should run their own Syncthing instance. Be aware that you might "
"need to configure listening ports such that they do not overlap (see :ref:"
"`config`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:198
msgid "Does Syncthing support syncing between folders on the same system?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:200
msgid ""
"No. Syncthing is not designed to sync locally and the overhead involved in "
"doing so using Syncthing's method would be wasteful. There are better "
"programs to achieve this such as rsync or Unison."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:205
msgid "Is Syncthing my ideal backup application?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:207
msgid ""
"No. Syncthing is not a great backup application because all changes to your "
"files (modifications, deletions, etc) will be propagated to all your "
"devices. You can enable versioning, but we encourage the use of other tools "
"to keep your data safe from your (or our) mistakes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:213
msgid "Why is there no iOS client?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:215
msgid ""
"There is an alternative implementation of Syncthing (using the same network "
"protocol) called ``fsync()``. There are no plans by the current Syncthing "
"team to support iOS in the foreseeable future, as the code required to do so "
"would be quite different from what Syncthing is today."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:221
msgid "How can I exclude files with brackets (``[]``) in the name?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:223
msgid ""
"The patterns in .stignore are glob patterns, where brackets are used to "
"denote character ranges. That is, the pattern ``q[abc]x`` will match the "
"files ``qax``, ``qbx`` and ``qcx``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:227
msgid ""
"To match an actual file *called* ``q[abc]x`` the pattern needs to \"escape\" "
"the brackets, like so: ``q\\[abc\\]x``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:230
msgid ""
"On Windows, escaping special characters is not supported as the ``\\`` "
"character is used as a path separator. On the other hand, special characters "
"such as ``[`` and ``?`` are not allowed in file names on Windows."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:235
msgid "Why is the setup more complicated than BTSync?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:237
msgid ""
"Security over convenience. In Syncthing you have to setup both sides to "
"connect two nodes. An attacker can't do much with a stolen node ID, because "
"you have to add the node on the other side too. You have better control "
"where your files are transferred."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:242
msgid "This is an area that we are working to improve in the long term."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:245
msgid "How do I access the web GUI from another computer?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:247
msgid ""
"The default listening address is 127.0.0.1:8384, so you can only access the "
"GUI from the same machine. This is for security reasons. Change the ``GUI "
"listen address`` through the web UI from ``127.0.0.1:8384`` to "
"``0.0.0.0:8384`` or change the config.xml:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:252
msgid ""
"<gui enabled=\"true\" tls=\"false\">\n"
"  <address>127.0.0.1:8384</address>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:257
msgid "to"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:259
msgid ""
"<gui enabled=\"true\" tls=\"false\">\n"
"  <address>0.0.0.0:8384</address>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:264
msgid ""
"Then the GUI is accessible from everywhere. You should set a password and "
"enable HTTPS with this configuration. You can do this from inside the GUI."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:267
msgid ""
"If both your computers are Unixy (Linux, Mac, etc) You can also leave the "
"GUI settings at default and use an ssh port forward to access it. For "
"example,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:271
msgid "$ ssh -L 9090:127.0.0.1:8384 user@othercomputer.example.com"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:275
msgid ""
"will log you into othercomputer.example.com, and present the *remote* "
"Syncthing GUI on http://localhost:9090 on your *local* computer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:279
msgid "Why do I see Syncthing twice in task manager?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:281
msgid ""
"One process manages the other, to capture logs and manage restarts. This "
"makes it easier to handle upgrades from within Syncthing itself, and also "
"ensures that we get a nice log file to help us narrow down the cause for "
"crashes and other bugs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:287
msgid "Where do Syncthing logs go to?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:289
msgid ""
"Syncthing logs to stdout by default. On Windows Syncthing by default also "
"creates ``syncthing.log`` in Syncthing's home directory (run ``syncthing -"
"paths`` to see where that is). Command line option ``-logfile`` can be used "
"to specify a user-defined logfile."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:295
msgid "How do I upgrade Syncthing?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:297
msgid ""
"If you use a package manager such as Debian's apt-get, you should upgrade "
"using the package manager. If you use the binary packages linked from "
"Syncthing.net, you can use Syncthing built in automatic upgrades."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:301
msgid ""
"If automatic upgrades is enabled (which is the default), Syncthing will "
"upgrade itself automatically within 24 hours of a new release."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:304
msgid ""
"The upgrade button appears in the web GUI when a new version has been "
"released. Pressing it will perform an upgrade."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:307
msgid "To force an upgrade from the command line, run ``syncthing -upgrade``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:309
msgid ""
"Note that your system should have CA certificates installed which allow a "
"secure connection to GitHub (e.g. FreeBSD requires ``sudo pkg install "
"ca_root_nss``). If ``curl`` or ``wget`` works with normal HTTPS sites, then "
"so should Syncthing."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:315
msgid "Where do I find the latest release?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/faq.rst:317
msgid ""
"We release new versions through GitHub. The latest release is always found "
"`on the release page <https://github.com/syncthing/syncthing/releases/"
"latest>`_. Unfortunately GitHub does not provide a single URL to "
"automatically download the latest version. We suggest to use the GitHub API "
"at https://api.github.com/repos/syncthing/syncthing/releases/latest and "
"parsing the JSON response."
msgstr ""
